  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 241 PM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nEarly this afternoon, GOES water vapor imagery shows an \nexpansive plume of deep Gulf moisture (PWATs ~1.5\"+) streaming \nnorthward from the Plains across the Upper Midwest and into \nOntario. This broad latitudinal transport is driven by a strong \nsouth- southwesterly low-level mass response east of a northern-\nstream trough traversing the Canadian Rockies and a southern- \nstream system advancing into the Desert Southwest. As the \nnorthern- stream trough moves rapidly east across southern \nCanada through midweek, it will push a surface cold front west- \nsouthwestward into the Midwest and western Great Lakes. \nMeanwhile, as the Desert Southwest trough moves toward the Great\nPlains through Thursday, it is forecast to eject several \nsmaller-scale impulses east-northeast over the Corn Belt into \nthe lower Great Lakes. These waves, combined with a persistent \nnorthward feed of Gulf moisture into the frontal boundary, will \nset the stage for multiple rounds of heavy rain Wednesday \nthrough Thursday night.\n\nInitial rain showers driven by warm air advection may move into\nportions of northwestern Illinois as early as later this \nevening. However, the first primary wave of rain is expected to \nshift into northern Illinois and parts of far northwestern \nIndiana late tonight (likely after 4:00 AM) and persist through \nthe morning. The threat of lightning with this activity appears \nlow (but non-zero), but locally heavier downpours may accompany\nit. Scattered shower activity will likely continue into \nWednesday afternoon and evening. The primary area of focus may \nbegin to shift northward toward the Wisconsin state line, though\nthe exact extent of this northward shift remains uncertain. \nSubstantial cloud cover and periods of rain will keep \ntemperatures in the 60s to around 70 across most of northern \nIllinois on Wednesday.\n\nThe axis of rainfall, potentially accompanied by some\nthunderstorms, is expected to shift back south into the area \nThursday into Thursday night as the surface frontal boundary \ndrops southward. The presence of near-record precipitable water\nvalues near 2 inches (rare for early October per sounding \nclimatology at ILX and DVN) suggests periods of moderate to \nheavy rainfall will accompany this system. Rainfall amounts of 2\nto 3 inches (with locally higher totals) are possible across \nthe area. This threat will be highlighted in a Hydrologic \nOutlook (ESF).\n\nThe surface cold front will shift south of the area on Friday, \nbringing an end to the wet pattern as high pressure builds in \nfor the weekend. Conditions for the weekend look dry and \ncooler\u2014more typical for early October\u2014with temperatures topping \nout in the 60s.\n\nKJB",
